    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "uaLaB",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -296,
      "name": "Title",
      "width": 1440,
      "height": 104,
      "content": "AGENTIC OS — BOARD CONCEPTS (wireframe)\nThree takes on the one hard problem (brief §3): keep CLAIMS (what a doc says) visually separate from FACTS (what git says now), and make the DELTA the loudest thing. Convention across all: sans + italic + quotes = a claim (provisional); monospace = a verified machine fact; status = colour + glyph + label. Real data measured 2026-07-26. 6 projects, no scroll @1440×900.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: ship B (Triage) as the default — it wins the 3-second scan and the ambient glance. Borrow C's drift-feed when many docs drift, and use A's claim→delta→fact gutter as the row-hover detail."
    },
    {
      "type": "note",
      "id": "WyLif",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 920,
      "name": "Rationale A",
      "width": 1400,
      "height": 150,
      "content": "CONCEPT A — LEDGER   ·   claim-vs-fact as SPATIAL separation.\nEach row reads left→right: CLAIM (sans, italic, aged) → DELTA (the gutter, loudest) → FACT (mono, crisp). The physical centre gutter with hairlines is the ‘gap where they disagree’. Heterogeneous rows solved by equal-height rows, content vertically centred, names/status holding the left scan-line.\n+ Optimises: the epistemic split is unmissable; the delta literally sits between claim and fact.\n− Sacrifices: tall rows = lower density (fills the screen at 6, won't scale past ~8); claim & fact columns compete for width."
    },
